# Code quality

Write code a teammate can change months later without a walkthrough.

- Name values for the role they play. A precise longer name beats a short vague one.
- One job per function. If a block needs a name to be clear, extract it.
- Keep control flow flat. Return early instead of nesting.
- Type inputs, outputs, and public data. No `any`, no unchecked casts.
- Handle failure where the caller can recover or report it. Never swallow an error.
- Remove unused code in the same change that makes it unused.
- Follow the structure already in the file before adding a new pattern.
- Prefer obvious data flow over clever abstraction.

Do not add comments, docstrings, file headers, TODOs, or commented out code. If a block needs a comment, rename or split it until the code states the point.

Add a package only when the platform or the current code cannot do the job cleanly.

Before installing:

- Use the official registry. No gists, raw URLs, unknown git repos, or mirrored tarballs.
- Use the real package name. Reject a name that is one character off a known package.
- Use a current stable release. No beta, nightly, alpha, or `*` range.
- Read `preinstall` and `postinstall` scripts first. Skip the package if those scripts download code or run a remote binary.
- Pin the version and commit the lockfile in the same change.

```solidity
function clampFee(uint24 fee, uint24 minFee, uint24 maxFee) internal pure returns (uint24) {
    if (fee < minFee) return minFee;
    if (fee > maxFee) return maxFee;
    return fee;
}
```
